-From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
-From: Zhiyu722 <Zhiyu722@users.noreply.github.com>
-Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 09:39:24 +0800
-Subject: [PATCH] input: hall: Add the BBK hall sensor framework
-
-This is the last missing piece of the hall subsystem: the framework that polls
-the up and the down hall sensor, keeps their recent samples in small ring
-buffers, recognises a mechanism that is pushed by hand, and carries the
-calibration data the Android daemon writes.
-
-Everything here comes from the factory binary, whose kallsyms still names the
-functions: bbk_hall_core_register_device, bbk_hall_core_read_data,
-bbk_hall_core_enable, bbk_hall_get_status, bbk_hall_core_set_delay,
-bbk_hall_core_init_queue, hall_clear_cali_data, the queue helpers and the
-misc device handler.
-
-  /dev/bbk_hall_core   misc device with four ioctls sharing the base
-                       0x40046000: SET_CALI and SET_MHALL_CALI persist their
-                       blob, TRANS_CALI and SET_MHALL_CALI_DAEMON only hand it
-                       to the kernel, and an unknown command is -ENOTTY while a
-                       failed copy is -EFAULT.
-  calibration files    /mnt/vendor/persist/sensors/cali_hall is exactly 56 bytes
-                       and cali_mhall_final exactly 48, both plain structures
-                       without a magic or a checksum, so the layouts in this
-                       file are the interface.  up_down_count is the text line
-                       "up-all" that TRANS_CALI reads back.
-  sysfs                /sys/devices/platform/bbk_hall_core/ exposes
-                       bbk_hall_delay, bbk_hall_enable, bbk_hall_data,
-                       bbk_hall_vendor, bbk_hall_cali_time and
-                       bbk_mhall_version with the vendor's semantics, including
-                       the 0.6 factor between the user and the internal unit of
-                       the calibration time.
-  registration         a sensor hands in a hall_dev whose name starts with "up"
-                       or "down"; once both are present the framework registers
-                       its own platform device and driver, exactly once.
-
-The MXM1120 pair now registers as the up and down sensors, which is what its
-device tree compatibles say and what the factory driver did, and the IST8801
-instances announce themselves through the older dhall_register_hall() path as
-the factory's IST8801 driver did.  The motor driver keeps its own weak
-definitions of the framework entry points, so either driver still links without
-the other; the weak stubs are deliberately not exported, because exporting the
-same name from two objects collides on the __crc_ symbol.
-
-Signed-off-by: Zhiyu722 <Zhiyu722@users.noreply.github.com>
